      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While congestion pricing is theoretically a powerful tool for reducing emissions, the reality is that it often fails to deliver the promised environmental wins because drivers simply switch to more polluting cars or ride-hailing services instead of taking transit. Cities that implement these schemes without a massive, immediate overhaul of public transit infrastructure end up with a net increase in total vehicle miles traveled and per-capita pollution. Therefore, calling it a \"no-brainer\" for climate goals is dangerous; it only works if the city simultaneously makes public transportation so attractive that people actually choose it over driving.",
